  "step_2": "The individual considers only the certainty of the outcome in Lottery A. The verbal report, 'Lieber den sicheren Gewinn mitnehmen' ('Better to take the certain gain'), indicates that the individual prioritized certainty and security. The deliberation focuses solely on the fact that Lottery A provides a guaranteed 3000 Euros, with no risk of loss. There is no mention of the distribution of probabilities, the magnitude of potential gains, the risk of losing, or any consideration of skewness. The individual does not refer to the 80% chance of 4000 Euros or the 20% chance of 0 Euros in Lottery B. The justification is based entirely on risk aversion and preference for certainty, not on the shape of the probability distribution.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness, specifically whether a lottery assigns a greater portion of probability to unfavorable outcomes. This involves comparing the probability mass on unfavorable versus favorable outcomes. The individual did not consider this aspect at all—there is no mention of probabilities, outcomes, or distributional shape. The justification is based on certainty, not on skewness. The information considered by the individual (certainty of outcome) does not align with the information required by the reason (skewness). Furthermore, the rule of the reason (preferring more right-skewed lotteries) is not reflected in the justification (preferring certainty). Therefore, the decision reason was not used. The individual’s deliberation is consistent with risk aversion or expected utility reasoning, but not with skewness-based evaluation.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
